{
	title: 'ChatGPT subscription: /login chatgpt and the OpenAI provider',
	spec: "ChatGPT subscribers can use Hal: `/login chatgpt` (alias openai) runs the old Hal's device-code flow (artifact old-auth-login.ts in task nd), which works on remote and headless hosts: the /login choice and direct login first link to https://chatgpt.com/#settings/Security and ask users to turn on Enable device code sign-in (Hal cannot inspect their setting), and a device-code 404 error links there too; it then shows the URL and code, waits up to 15 min without blocking the host, and writes the tokens to this home's credentials file as an openai account; tokens refresh like anthropic's. An `openai` provider speaks the Responses API as the old Hal did (artifact old-openai.ts): a ChatGPT OAuth token without api.responses.write goes to the Codex backend (chatgpt.com/backend-api/codex/responses) with its account id; an OPENAI_API_KEY goes to api.openai.com. Streaming text, reasoning, tool calls, images, usage and stop reasons map to Hal's provider events like anthropic's do; failures carry the same failure kinds (auth, temporary, limited with retryAt from resets_in_seconds), so rotation and blocked-on-login work unchanged. The model list of a ChatGPT login is the Codex backend's own (GET chatgpt.com/backend-api/codex/models?client_version=99.0.0, models with visibility 'list'; a low client_version hides newer models), asked at host startup and cached like other provider lists, because models.dev lists API-only models that fail there with a 400 ('gpt-5.6', 'gpt-6-terra'). An API key, or a failed request, gets models.dev's ids (task qq) plus the old Hal's known GPT ids. HTTP streaming (SSE) only; the old WebSocket transport is a later optimisation. A ChatGPT subscription token's context window is 272k input (old Hal: subscriptionContextWindow). On the host's own machine (no webUrl configured, host not started over SSH) /login chatgpt uses the Codex CLI's browser flow instead, which needs no device-code setting: PKCE with a random state, a listener on 127.0.0.1:1455 (the only redirect OpenAI accepts: http://localhost:1455/auth/callback), the authorize URL shown to open, the code exchanged with that redirect; a wrong state is refused, it times out after 15 minutes, and a busy port falls back to the device code. The /login choice then omits the device-code hint. Test: a fake redirect with the wrong state is refused, the right one saves the tokens.",
	status: 'done',
	notes: [
		"auth.ts is generic over Kind ('anthropic' | 'openai'): auth.pick(kind, model) with auth.anthropic/auth.openai as wrappers; all/accounts/credential/refresh/rejected take the kind (default anthropic). The file key is the kind, the env key ANTHROPIC_API_KEY / OPENAI_API_KEY (./test unsets OPENAI_API_KEY too). auth.tokenUrl(kind) is one override in tests for both kinds. OpenAI refresh is the Codex CLI's JSON request (client_id, grant_type, refresh_token, scope 'openid profile email'); the old Hal's refresh code was not an artifact, so this is from memory of Codex and unverified live.",
		"The device-code flow (login-chatgpt.ts) runs inside the command's async run (slash.runCommand is not awaited by the host), so nothing blocks; Context.say shows the URL and code before the wait. The poll lives in memory: a restart drops it and the user runs /login chatgpt again. The browser is not opened (headless hosts). login.save(entry, kind) matches openai entries by ChatGPT accountId, anthropic ones by email; the account's name is its email from the JWT profile claim.",
		"openai.ts replays reasoning as { type: 'reasoning', summary rebuilt from the block text, encrypted_content } with no id: store is false, and the signature is JSON { encrypted_content }. Thinking from other providers is dropped, like anthropic.ts does (the old Hal replayed it as text). reasoning { summary: 'auto' } and include encrypted content go to models matching gpt-5/o<n>/codex; openai.effort(model) is undefined (model default) until a thinking-effort setting exists. The Codex backend gets text.verbosity 'high' as the old Hal sent and never max_output_tokens. ProviderRequest.sessionId becomes prompt_cache_key.",
		"The picker's fallback is now models.dev's ids plus Provider.known() (models.fallback), not known() only when models.dev has none, so the old Hal's GPT ids (openai.knownModels) always show. Provider.contextWindow?(model) lets a provider cap models.contextWindow: openai caps at 272k when the first openai account is a token without api.responses.write (for every model, not only the old Hal's gpt-5.5/5.6 list).",
		'Not verified against the live Codex backend or api.openai.com at implementation time (no real credentials in tests). The first real /login chatgpt and request should be checked by hand: device endpoints, the exchange, the refresh body, reasoning replay without id.',
		'Verified live 29 Sep: the Codex models list had gpt-6.1-sol, gpt-6-sol/astra/luna, gpt-5.6-sol/terra/luna and gpt-5.5 (all answered a request) plus hidden gpt-reserve and codex-auto-review; gpt-6-terra and bare gpt-5.6 were refused with 400. client_version 0.99.0 returned almost nothing, 0.140.0 only gpt-5.5.'
	],
	needs: ['nd', 'qq']
}
